1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,682
<font color="#000000">Graph neural networks are increasingly deployed in
safety-critical settings,</font>

2
00:00:04,762 --> 00:00:08,944
<font color="#000000">but their confidence estimates often become
unreliable when the test data shifts away from training.</font>

3
00:00:09,024 --> 00:00:13,206
<font color="#000000">This paper,</font>

4
00:00:13,286 --> 00:00:17,468
<font color="#000000">from the University of Michigan and Lawrence
Livermore National Laboratory,</font>

5
00:00:17,548 --> 00:00:21,730
<font color="#000000">introduces G-Delta-UQ,</font>

6
00:00:21,810 --> 00:00:25,992
<font color="#000000">a single-model method for estimating epistemic
uncertainty in graph neural networks.</font>

7
00:00:26,072 --> 00:00:30,254
<font color="#000000">By adapting stochastic centering and partial
stochasticity to structured graph data,</font>

8
00:00:30,334 --> 00:00:34,516
<font color="#000000">G-Delta-UQ produces better-calibrated confidence
indicators across size, concept,</font>

9
00:00:34,596 --> 00:00:38,778
<font color="#000000">and covariate distribution shifts,</font>

10
00:00:38,858 --> 00:00:43,040
<font color="#000000">and improves downstream tasks like
out-of-distribution detection and generalization gap prediction.</font>

11
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:49,004
<font color="#000000">Graph neural networks are being deployed in
high-stakes applications where the test-time</font>

12
00:00:49,084 --> 00:00:51,579
<font color="#000000">data rarely matches training conditions.</font>

13
00:00:51,659 --> 00:00:56,664
<font color="#000000">In these shifted settings, downstream safety
metrics such as calibration error,</font>

14
00:00:56,744 --> 00:00:58,595
<font color="#000000">out-of-distribution rejection,</font>

15
00:00:58,675 --> 00:01:03,744
<font color="#000000">and generalization gap prediction all rely on the
model's confidence indicators.</font>

16
00:01:03,824 --> 00:01:11,018
<font color="#000000">While the computer vision community has long known
that confidence quality deteriorates under distribution shift,</font>

17
00:01:11,098 --> 00:01:15,395
<font color="#000000">this behavior has remained under-explored for graph
neural networks.</font>

18
00:01:15,475 --> 00:01:21,703
<font color="#000000">This paper asks whether GNN confidence indicators
can be made reliable under realistic structural,</font>

19
00:01:21,783 --> 00:01:24,020
<font color="#000000">size, concept, and covariate shifts.</font>

20
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:29,671
<font color="#000000">There is a common expectation that adopting more
advanced or expressive architectures</font>

21
00:01:29,751 --> 00:01:31,937
<font color="#000000">will inherently improve calibration.</font>

22
00:01:32,017 --> 00:01:36,280
<font color="#000000">Through a controlled case study on a structural
distortion benchmark,</font>

23
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:42,197
<font color="#000000">the authors demonstrate this expectation is false:
graph transformers and positional encodings</font>

24
00:01:42,277 --> 00:01:47,107
<font color="#000000">do not meaningfully improve calibration over
vanilla message-passing networks,</font>

25
00:01:47,187 --> 00:01:50,758
<font color="#000000">and increasing model size can even make calibration worse.</font>

26
00:01:50,838 --> 00:01:53,276
<font color="#000000">This finding motivates a different path.</font>

27
00:01:53,356 --> 00:01:55,353
<font color="#000000">Rather than chasing expressivity,</font>

28
00:01:55,433 --> 00:02:01,900
<font color="#000000">the paper advocates for epistemic uncertainty
quantification to directly modulate confidence indicators.</font>

29
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:05,929
<font color="#000000">The paper makes three contributions.</font>

30
00:02:06,009 --> 00:02:09,658
<font color="#000000">First,</font>

31
00:02:09,738 --> 00:02:13,387
<font color="#000000">a rigorous case study establishing that improving
graph neural network expressivity does</font>

32
00:02:13,467 --> 00:02:17,116
<font color="#000000">not mitigate poor calibration under distribution shift.</font>

33
00:02:17,196 --> 00:02:20,845
<font color="#000000">Second, G-Delta-UQ,</font>

34
00:02:20,925 --> 00:02:24,575
<font color="#000000">a novel single-model uncertainty method that
extends the stochastic centering framework to structured graph data and,</font>

35
00:02:24,655 --> 00:02:28,304
<font color="#000000">crucially,</font>

36
00:02:28,384 --> 00:02:32,033
<font color="#000000">supports partial stochasticity so that only part of
the network is made stochastic.</font>

37
00:02:32,113 --> 00:02:35,762
<font color="#000000">Third, an extensive empirical evaluation spanning
covariate, concept,</font>

38
00:02:35,842 --> 00:02:39,491
<font color="#000000">and graph size shifts, across the safety-critical
tasks of calibration,</font>

39
00:02:39,571 --> 00:02:43,220
<font color="#000000">generalization gap prediction, and
out-of-distribution detection.</font>

40
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:47,703
<font color="#000000">G-Delta-UQ builds on the principle of stochastic
anchoring.</font>

41
00:02:47,783 --> 00:02:51,885
<font color="#000000">Each input sample is transformed into a relative
representation by subtracting a randomly</font>

42
00:02:51,965 --> 00:02:56,068
<font color="#000000">chosen anchor and concatenating it channel-wise,</font>

43
00:02:56,148 --> 00:03:00,251
<font color="#000000">producing an anchored input.</font>

44
00:03:00,331 --> 00:03:04,433
<font color="#000000">By varying the anchor across iterations,</font>

45
00:03:04,513 --> 00:03:08,616
<font color="#000000">the model emulates sampling from a distribution of
hypotheses,</font>

46
00:03:08,696 --> 00:03:12,799
<font color="#000000">and the variance of predictions over multiple
anchors captures epistemic uncertainty.</font>

47
00:03:12,879 --> 00:03:16,981
<font color="#000000">Because directly anchoring on graph adjacency would
introduce artificial edges,</font>

48
00:03:17,061 --> 00:03:21,164
<font color="#000000">the authors anchor in node-feature or
hidden-representation space.</font>

49
00:03:21,244 --> 00:03:25,347
<font color="#000000">They introduce three variants, applying anchoring
at the node features,</font>

50
00:03:25,427 --> 00:03:29,529
<font color="#000000">at an intermediate message-passing layer, or after
the readout.</font>

51
00:03:29,609 --> 00:03:33,712
<font color="#000000">A particularly practical variant freezes a
pretrained backbone and only trains an anchored classifier head,</font>

52
00:03:33,792 --> 00:03:37,895
<font color="#000000">delivering inexpensive, partial stochasticity.</font>

53
00:03:37,975 --> 00:03:42,077
<font color="#000000">At inference,</font>

54
00:03:42,157 --> 00:03:46,260
<font color="#000000">predictions are averaged over K anchors to produce
both a calibrated prediction and an uncertainty estimate.</font>

55
00:03:46,640 --> 00:03:49,245
<font color="#000000">The evaluation is deliberately broad.</font>

56
00:03:49,325 --> 00:03:50,769
<font color="#000000">For structural shift,</font>

57
00:03:50,849 --> 00:03:58,897
<font color="#000000">the authors build a Rotated Super-pixel MNIST
benchmark where increasing rotation induces controlled distortion.</font>

58
00:03:58,977 --> 00:04:04,848
<font color="#000000">For size shift, they use standard graph
classification datasets including D and D,</font>

59
00:04:04,928 --> 00:04:09,202
<font color="#000000">NCI1, NCI109, and PROTEINS with GCN, GIN, and PNA
backbones.</font>

60
00:04:09,282 --> 00:04:11,597
<font color="#000000">For concept and covariate shifts,</font>

61
00:04:11,677 --> 00:04:17,620
<font color="#000000">they use the GOOD benchmark suite covering colored
MNIST, motif, and SST2 datasets.</font>

62
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:24,514
<font color="#000000">Across all of these they compare against strong
uncertainty baselines including deep ensembles,</font>

63
00:04:24,594 --> 00:04:29,884
<font color="#000000">temperature scaling, and Monte Carlo dropout,
measuring calibration error,</font>

64
00:04:29,964 --> 00:04:35,690
<font color="#000000">out-of-distribution detection AUROC, and
generalization gap mean absolute error.</font>

65
00:04:36,070 --> 00:04:40,075
<font color="#000000">Across the board, G-Delta-UQ improves the
reliability of confidence indicators.</font>

66
00:04:40,155 --> 00:04:44,161
<font color="#000000">On the structural distortion benchmark it achieves
substantial reductions in expected</font>

67
00:04:44,241 --> 00:04:48,246
<font color="#000000">calibration error even at severe rotations,</font>

68
00:04:48,326 --> 00:04:52,332
<font color="#000000">while keeping accuracy close to the vanilla model.</font>

69
00:04:52,412 --> 00:04:56,417
<font color="#000000">Under size shift,</font>

70
00:04:56,497 --> 00:05:00,502
<font color="#000000">last-layer anchoring lowers calibration error while
maintaining or improving accuracy,</font>

71
00:05:00,582 --> 00:05:04,588
<font color="#000000">with the largest gains on the most severe shift.</font>

72
00:05:04,668 --> 00:05:08,673
<font color="#000000">Under concept and covariate shifts it delivers
competitive in-distribution and out-of-distribution</font>

73
00:05:08,753 --> 00:05:12,758
<font color="#000000">accuracy alongside better calibration than other
single-model methods.</font>

74
00:05:12,838 --> 00:05:16,844
<font color="#000000">And when its confidence estimates feed downstream tasks,</font>

75
00:05:16,924 --> 00:05:20,929
<font color="#000000">it is among the best single-model estimators for
generalization gap prediction and is</font>

76
00:05:21,009 --> 00:05:25,015
<font color="#000000">highly competitive for out-of-distribution detection,</font>

77
00:05:25,095 --> 00:05:29,100
<font color="#000000">with the pretrained variant frequently the
strongest under covariate shift.</font>

78
00:05:29,480 --> 00:05:33,890
<font color="#000000">The paper carefully ablates where stochastic
anchoring should be applied.</font>

79
00:05:33,970 --> 00:05:37,641
<font color="#000000">On the D and D dataset, which has the most severe
size shift,</font>

80
00:05:37,721 --> 00:05:44,899
<font color="#000000">applying anchoring after the readout layer
dramatically improves both accuracy and calibration as network depth grows,</font>

81
00:05:44,979 --> 00:05:47,974
<font color="#000000">while earlier-layer anchoring converges less well.</font>

82
00:05:48,054 --> 00:05:53,694
<font color="#000000">This leads to a practical recommendation to anchor
at the last layer for size-shift settings.</font>

83
00:05:53,774 --> 00:05:56,954
<font color="#000000">The authors also validate that partial stochasticity,</font>

84
00:05:57,034 --> 00:06:02,058
<font color="#000000">in the form of the pretrained variant that only
trains an anchored classifier head,</font>

85
00:06:02,138 --> 00:06:03,965
<font color="#000000">is both effective and scalable,</font>

86
00:06:04,045 --> 00:06:09,193
<font color="#000000">and a neural tangent kernel analysis confirms that
intermediate constant shifts truly</font>

87
00:06:09,273 --> 00:06:13,990
<font color="#000000">alter the model's function rather than acting as a
trivial reparameterization.</font>

88
00:06:14,370 --> 00:06:18,867
<font color="#FFFFFF">In numbers,</font>

89
00:06:18,947 --> 00:06:23,444
<font color="#FFFFFF">G-Delta-UQ is a single-model method offering three
anchoring variants plus a pretrained partial-stochasticity option.</font>

90
00:06:23,524 --> 00:06:28,021
<font color="#FFFFFF">It is evaluated across three distinct types of
distribution shift and three safety-critical downstream tasks,</font>

91
00:06:28,101 --> 00:06:32,599
<font color="#FFFFFF">and compared against four established uncertainty
baselines including deep ensembles.</font>

92
00:06:32,679 --> 00:06:37,176
<font color="#FFFFFF">Despite requiring only a single model,</font>

93
00:06:37,256 --> 00:06:41,753
<font color="#FFFFFF">it consistently matches or outperforms these
baselines on calibration,</font>

94
00:06:41,833 --> 00:06:46,330
<font color="#FFFFFF">out-of-distribution detection, and generalization
gap prediction.</font>

95
00:06:46,710 --> 00:06:48,457
<font color="#FFFFFF">The core message is simple.</font>

96
00:06:48,537 --> 00:06:54,887
<font color="#FFFFFF">Do not expect larger or more expressive graph
neural networks to automatically give trustworthy</font>

97
00:06:54,967 --> 00:06:58,000
<font color="#FFFFFF">confidence estimates under distribution shift.</font>

98
00:06:58,080 --> 00:07:02,264
<font color="#FFFFFF">Instead, adapt the principle of stochastic
anchoring to graphs.</font>

99
00:07:02,344 --> 00:07:07,813
<font color="#FFFFFF">G-Delta-UQ is a flexible, scalable, single-model
framework that produces reliable,</font>

100
00:07:07,893 --> 00:07:13,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">well-calibrated uncertainty across structural,
size, concept, and covariate shifts,</font>

101
00:07:13,510 --> 00:07:18,980
<font color="#FFFFFF">and its pretrained variant makes it inexpensive to
add to models you already have.</font>
